    24	**ABSTRACT**
    25	
    26	
    27	Keeping dependencies up to date is a crucial software maintenance
    28	task that requires significant effort. Developers must choose which
    29	dependencies to update, select appropriate target versions, and
    30	minimize the impact of updates in terms of breaking changes and
    31	incompatibilities. Several factors influence the choice of a new
    32	dependency version, including its freshness, popularity, absence of
    33	vulnerabilities, and compatibility.
    34	In this paper, we formulate the dependency update problem
    35	as a multi-objective optimization problem. This approach allows
    36	for updating dependencies with a global perspective, considering
    37	all direct and indirect dependencies. It also enables developers to
    38	specify their preferences regarding the quality factors to maximize
    39	and the costs to minimize when updating. The update problem is
    40	encoded as a linear program whose solution provides an optimal
    41	update strategy that aligns with developer priorities and minimizes
    42	incompatibilities.
    43	We evaluate our approach using a dataset of 107 well-tested
    44	open-source Java projects using various configurations that reflect
    45	real-world update scenarios and consider three quality metrics: dependency freshness, a time-window popularity measure, and a
    46	vulnerability score related to CVEs. Our findings indicate that our
    47	approach generates updates that compile and pass tests as well as
    48	the naive approaches typically implemented in dependency bots.
    49	Furthermore, our approach can be up to two orders of magnitude
    50	better in terms of freshness. By considering a more comprehensive
    51	concept of quality debt, which accounts for freshness, popularity,
    52	and vulnerabilities, our approach is able to reduce quality debt
    53	while maintaining reasonable memory and time consumption.
