   110	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
   111	
   112	
   113	Leveraging the time-honored principles of modularity and reuse,
   114	modern software systems development typically entails using external _software libraries_ . Instead of creating new systems from scratch,
   115	developers incorporate libraries that provide the desired functionalities into their projects. These libraries expose their features through
   116	Application Programming Interfaces (APIs), which dictate the interactions between client projects and libraries. The way dependencies are managed varies across different software ecosystems [ 9 ].
   117	Typically, it involves using a package manager or build system
   118	to automatically retrieve specific versions of dependencies from
   119	remote software repositories—along with their own (transitive)
   120	dependencies—in order to build a so-called _dependency graph_ [ 28 ].
   121	For example, JavaScript and TypeScript developers can use npm
   122	or Yarn to fetch dependencies from the npm registry, while Java
   123	developers can use Maven or Gradle to retrieve dependencies from
   124	the Maven Central repository.
   125	Libraries continuously evolve to incorporate new features, bug
   126	fixes, security patches, refactorings, _etc_ . [ 1, 4 ]. Clients must stay
   127	up to date with the libraries they use to benefit from these improvements and to avoid technical lag and the associated technical
   128	debt [ 5, 11, 29 ]. However, when a library evolves, it may introduce
   129	changes that break the contract previously established with its
   130	clients, resulting in syntactic and semantic errors [ 35 ]. Developers
   131	are thus faced with the challenge of maximizing the freshness and
   132	quality of their dependencies while minimizing the costs associated
   133	with updating them. This challenge is further complicated by the
   134	nature of dependency graphs: updating a single dependency can
   135	
