   142	
   143	
   144	
   145	cause a snowball effect and result in incompatibilities with other
   146	indirect dependencies. As a result, clients sometimes hesitate to update their dependencies, raising security concerns [ 21 ] and making
   147	future updates even more difficult.
   148	The problem of assisting developers in updating their dependencies has therefore attracted significant interest. This includes efforts
   149	to identify client code that is affected by breaking changes [ 24, 25 ],
   150	automatically migrate client code [ 36 ], or find versions that minimize the impact on the dependency graph. UPCY [ 8 ], in particular,
   151	is a novel approach that takes a library and a target version as
   152	input to construct a migration plan that minimizes the number of
   153	breaking changes within the graph induced by the update. While
   154	this approach is particularly suitable for updating a single dependency to a specific version ( _e_ . _g_ ., to avoid a particular vulnerability,
   155	as exemplified by the recent log4shell mayhem), it is much less
   156	suitable for updating the entire dependency graph at once, which
   157	is critical for managing technical debt in decaying projects. Besides,
   158	breaking changes may not accurately reflect the actual impact an
   159	update has, as it has been empirically shown that many breaking
   160	releases do not impact client projects in practice [19, 25].
   161	Migrating to the latest available version of each dependency
   162	may not always be the optimal choice. Developers must juggle
   163	various criteria to find a satisfactory solution, ranging from ensuring license consistency across projects [ 32 ] to minimizing security
   164	vulnerabilities [ 21 ] and easing the migration process [ 25 ]. In this
   165	paper, we propose to model the problem of updating a dependency
   166	graph as a custom multi-objective optimization problem. We formulate the optimization problem as a linear programming problem
   167	on a project-rooted extended dependency graph. Our approach
   168	is generic regarding the quality and cost metrics considered. As
   169	different developers prioritize these criteria differently, our multiobjective problem incorporates weights for each, hence supporting
   170	updates tailored to organizational rules or individual developers’
   171	preferences. While other criteria can be considered, we focus in
   172	our experimental evaluation on the joint use of three quality metrics: dependency freshness (to minimize the cost of future updates),
   173	a time-window popularity measure (as a proxy for community
   174	support), and a vulnerability score based on CVEs (as a proxy for
   175	security concerns). For the cost of change, we estimate the impact
   176	breaking changes introduced in a release have on the project.
   177	We develop a new tool, GoblinUpdater, that automatically proposes an update plan from developer-defined preferences. GoblinUpdater targets the Java programming language and the Maven
   178	ecosystem and leverages the Maven Dependency Graph [ 2 ] and
   179	the enrichment capabilities offered by Goblin [ 16 ] to incorporate
   180	quality and cost metrics into dependency graphs. GoblinUpdater
   181	also leverages Maracas to mesure the impact of breaking changes
   182	on client code [ 25 ]. Our experiments evaluate the correctness, effectiveness, and scalability of GoblinUpdater on a dataset of 107
   183	well-tested open-source Maven projects. We show that GoblinUpdater outperforms naive approaches while maintaining reasonable
   184	memory and time consumption.
   185	The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2
   186	introduces the problem of balancing the quality and cost of dependency updates and the existing approaches. Section 3 gives a general
   187	overview of our approach, Section 4 discusses the construction of
   188	project-rooted dependency graphs, and Section 5 the encoding of
