   213	**2** **PROBLEM STATEMENT & RELATED WORK**
   214	
   215	
   216	To illustrate the challenges of balancing the quality and cost of updating dependencies, consider the dependency graph _𝐺_ shown in
   217	Figure 1. The figure depicts a simple root project _𝑝_ that declares two
   218	direct dependencies towards libraries l1 (in version l1-1) and l2 (in
   219	version l2-1), and inherits indirect dependencies towards libraries
   220	l3–l4. Each library offers a set of releases that act as candidates
   221	for replacing existing dependency versions (l1, for instance, offers
   222	releases l1-1, l1-2, and l1-3, with l-3 the most recent release). Migrating from one library version to another incurs a change cost,
   223	depicted with dotted arrows in Figure 1.
   224	Updating the dependencies of project _𝑝_ involves finding a subgraph _𝐺_ [′] of _𝐺_ that satisfies ecosystem-specific well-formedness
   225	constraints ( _e_ . _g_ ., one can only directly depend on a single version
   226	of a given library), maximizes the quality of each dependency in
   227	the graph rooted in _𝑝_, and minimizes the cost of migrating to new
