   238	versions. A given solution must specify the version of each dependency, whether direct or transitive, to ensure that no version is left
   239	open for the dependency resolver to pick arbitrarily. The goal is
   240	to find an optimal solution with respect to specific user-defined
   241	quality and cost preferences. Even in the simple example of Figure 1, combining every candidate version of each library yields
   242	ten candidate solutions _𝐺_ [′] . In real-world settings with dozens or
   243	hundreds of dependencies, the number of possible solutions quickly
   244	becomes unmanageable with naive algorithms and approaches.
   245	Several factors can be considered when selecting a dependency.
   246	These can range from its freshness and the compatibility of its
   247	license with the project, to the absence of known security vulnerabilities (CVEs) or its overall popularity and community support. In
   248	this paper, we choose to focus on three quality metrics: freshness,
   249	popularity, and the number of known vulnerabilities. We choose
   250	these metrics because they have been used in prior work and reflect
   251	real-world concerns [ 21, 32 ]. However, our approach is both generic
   252	and extensible, allowing for the integration of additional criteria,
   253	such as licensing constraints, into the solution.
   254	In Figure 1, consider the simple case where a developer wants to
   255	maximize the freshness of their dependencies while minimizing the
   256	cost of migrating to these versions. Updating would mean choosing
   257	{l1-3, l2-2, l5-1, l6-2}—assuming that these do not incur significant
   258	costs in terms of breaking changes. Another developer may choose
   259	to prioritize maximizing the freshness of dependencies and minimizing the presence of vulnerabilities, regardless of the cost of
   260	migrating to this new configuration. A successful approach should
   261	enable developers to (i) express their quality and cost preferences
   262	precisely and (ii) determine an optimal solution based on these
   263	preferences within a reasonable time.
   264	Several tools have been developed to assist developers in managing their dependencies ( _e_ . _g_ ., Dependabot [ 13 ], Renovate [ 26 ],
   265	and Greenkeeper [ 27 ]). These tools monitor the release of new dependencies and automatically create pull requests submitted for
   266	approval to project maintainers. They operate at the level of individual releases and do not aim to find a global solution that satisfies
   267	user-defined criteria. UPCY [ 8 ], on the other hand, is a novel approach to dependency update that takes as input one library to
   268	update and one target version to construct a migration plan that
   269	minimizes the number of breaking changes induced by version
   270	updates across the entire dependency graph. While this approach is
   271	well-suited for updating a single dependency to a specific version
   272	( _e_ . _g_ ., to avoid a specific vulnerability), it is much less suitable for
   273	updating the entire dependency graph at once. Besides, the number
   274	of breaking changes is a poor indicator of the actual impact of an update, as it has been shown empirically that many breaking releases
   275	have no practical impact on client projects [ 19, 25 ]. In contrast, our
   276	approach considers the _impact_ of breaking changes on the root
   277	project _𝑝_ and allows for updating all project dependencies simultaneously. Hejderup et al. studied to which extent test suites of client
   278	projects can detect regression caused by dependency updates and
   279	found that tests can only detect 47% of artificial faults injected in direct dependencies and 35% of those injected in transitive ones [ 14 ].
   280	They advocate that a successful approach to dependency update
   281	should incorporate static analysis to compensate the inadequacy of
   282	tests. Our approach follows this path by incorporating the precise
   283	cost of dependency updates directly within the dependency graph<response clipped><NOTE>To save on context only part of this file has been shown to you. You should retry this tool after you have searched inside the file with `grep -n` in order to find the line numbers of what you are looking for.</NOTE>


